---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import GuideLayout from '../../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';

// Captured from the real Control Panel by scripts/capture-guide-screenshots.mjs.
// Imported rather than referenced from public/ so astro:assets derives the
// dimensions and emits a responsive, optimised image.
import shotClassrooms from '../../../assets/guide/01-classrooms.png';
import shotRoster from '../../../assets/guide/02-roster.png';
import shotFormEmpty from '../../../assets/guide/03-refer-modal-empty.png';
import shotFormFilled from '../../../assets/guide/04-refer-modal-filled.png';
import shotMyReferrals from '../../../assets/guide/05-my-referrals.png';
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="refer-a-student"
  title="Refer a student to the counselling team"
  description="When you notice a student struggling, a referral puts them in front of your school's counselling team. It takes about a minute."
  audience="For teachers"
  readingTime="3 min read"
>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Open Classrooms</h2>
      <p>
        Sign in to the Control Panel and you will land on <strong>Classrooms</strong>. Every class
        you teach is here, with the number of students and any referrals that are still open.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>Home teacher</strong> badge marks the classes where you are the home teacher —
        those are the ones where you also approve transfers in and out.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={shotClassrooms}
          alt="The Classrooms screen showing three class cards: Grade 9 - A with one open referral and a Home teacher badge, Grade 9 - B, and Grade 10 - A."
          loading="eager"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Your classes. The count under each name is the number of students; open referrals are
          flagged on the card.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Open the class and find the student</h2>
      <p>
        Click a class to see its roster. The <strong>Referral</strong> column tells you where each
        student already stands, so you are never guessing whether someone has been flagged.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>—</strong> means nobody has referred this student.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Referred</strong> with a status means there is already an open case. If you filed
          it, you will see <strong>View</strong> instead of <strong>Refer</strong>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        In a hurry, use the search box at the top right — it searches every student across all the
        classes you teach, and nothing beyond them.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={shotRoster}
          alt="The Grade 9 - A roster listing six students. Kabir Menon shows a Referred, In progress chip and a View button; the rest show a dash and a Refer button."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Kabir Menon already has an open referral, so his row offers <strong>View</strong>. The
          others offer <strong>Refer</strong>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Click Refer</h2>
      <p>
        The referral form opens with the student's name and class already filled in. There are three
        things to give it, and all three are quick.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={shotFormEmpty}
          alt="The empty referral form titled Refer to the help group, for Aarav Sharma in Grade 9 - A, with a What have you noticed field, concern chips, and three urgency options."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          The form opens on the student you picked — you cannot refer the wrong child by accident.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Describe what you have seen</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>What have you noticed?</strong> is the part that matters most. Write what you
        actually observed and roughly when it started. Twenty characters is the minimum; two or
        three sentences is usually right.
      </p>

      <div class="guide-note">
        <p>
          <strong>Describe behaviour, not diagnosis.</strong> "Has stopped sitting with his usual
          group and handed in two incomplete assignments" is useful to a counsellor. "Has anxiety"
          is not — it is a clinical judgement, and it is theirs to make.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Then pick up to four <strong>concerns</strong> and set <strong>how soon</strong> it needs
        looking at:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Routine</strong> — reviewed within a week.</li>
        <li><strong>Soon</strong> — reviewed in a day or two.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Urgent</strong> — goes to the top of the counselling team's queue. Use it when you
          mean it, so it keeps working.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="guide-note guide-privacy">
        <p>
          <strong>Who sees this.</strong> The counselling team sees the student's name, class and
          exactly what you write. Other teachers do not. If a student is in immediate danger, file
          the referral <em>and</em> follow your school's emergency protocol — a referral is not an
          emergency alarm.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <Image
          src={shotFormFilled}
          alt="The referral form completed: a three-sentence description of what the teacher noticed, the Academic decline and Withdrawn / isolated concerns selected, and Soon chosen for urgency."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          A good referral: concrete observations, a rough start date, two concerns, and an honest
          urgency. Then press <strong>Send referral</strong>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Follow it up under My Referrals</h2>
      <p>
        Everything you have filed lives under <strong>My Referrals</strong>, with its current status.
        You do not need to chase anyone — the status moves as the counselling team picks it up.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pending</strong> — filed, not yet picked up.</li>
        <li><strong>Acknowledged</strong> — a counsellor has it.</li>
        <li><strong>In progress</strong> — they are working with the student.</li>
        <li><strong>Closed</strong> — the case is finished.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When a case closes you may get a short summary from the counsellor. That summary is
        deliberately brief: it tells you what you need in order to support the student in class,
        without handing over the contents of their sessions.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={shotMyReferrals}
          alt="The My Referrals tab listing two referrals — one in progress for Kabir Menon, and one closed for Saanvi Reddy with a short summary from the counsellor."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Your own referrals only. You will not see referrals filed by other teachers, even for
          students you teach.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-footer">
    <h2>Things worth knowing</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>One open referral per student.</strong> If a colleague has already referred the same
      student, you will be told rather than creating a duplicate — two teachers noticing the same
      child should land on one case, not two.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A referral follows the student.</strong> If they move class mid-term, the referral stays
      with you as the person who filed it, and the counsellor is told about the move.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>You cannot see a student's counselling notes</strong>, and that is on purpose. You see
      the status of your referral and whatever summary the counsellor chooses to share with you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stuck? Email <a href="mailto:admin@zengarden.co.in">admin@zengarden.co.in</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
